{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01CsspFN3dmB3k76ZeivMFt6","type":"tool_result","content":"<persisted-output>\nOutput too large (88.5KB). Full output saved to: /home/go/.claude/projects/-var-tmp-pose-work-go-pose-review-run-2171335808-worktree/[private-session]/tool-results/bl1t9p3xm.txt\n\nPreview (first 2KB):\n---\nslug: pose-index-recovery-and-close-scope-consistency\nstatus: in-progress\ncreated_at: 2026-11-10\ncompleted_at:\nsupersedes:\ndepends_on:\nremediates:\npriority: 1\ncomponents: pose-mcp\ntask_type: bugfix\ndelivers: governance:index-recovery-and-close-scope-consistency\n---\n\n# Spec: O motor aponta o remédio que funciona: índice indisponível, escopo do `close` e limite do módulo\n\n## 1. Intent\n\n### Goal\n\nTrês respostas do motor mandavam o operador para um comando que não resolvia o que ele acabara de tentar. Cada uma vira uma resposta consistente: o que os leitores recomendam (`pose index`) repara; o `pose close` de um escopo que não é spec diz como ele fecha; e o índice de módulos não conta contra o seu limite o registro de governança que cresce a cada fechamento.\n\n### Business value\n\nAchado ao investigar o aviso persistente do harne8 (113 avisos do gate, todos \"review input closure is unknown: index generation does not bind input …\"), 2026-11-10:\n\n1. O harne8 tinha um commit (`e1593bde`) com `spec-graph.json` e `roadmaps.json` regenerados e o `generation.json` não; os leitores respondiam \"does not match generation …; run `pose index`\" e o `pose index` recusava (`index input closure could not be proved … index generation could not be pinned; nothing was written`): a operação que constrói o índice a partir das fontes tratava como desconhecida a geração que ela mesma vai substituir.\n2. Mesmo corrigido isso, o `pose index` do harne8 falhava com \"module discovery walk: governance read view budget exceeded\": o limite de 10 000 entradas da varredura de módulos foi cruzado por 7 319 arquivos rastreados do submódulo `pose-dist` (1 652 bundles e 1 597 atestados de review, 525 specs, …) mais os 2 833 do próprio harne8; o `.pose` de cada projeto não contém módulo e cresce a cada fechamento, então uma instância grande nunca mais atualizaria seus índices. A mensagem era só o nome do orçamento.\n...\n</persisted-output>","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"[private-session]","uuid":"8ea5873b-467e-4555-a9cb-8e3be55b1419","timestamp":"2026-11-10T14:20:40.871Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"---\nslug: pose-index-recovery-and-close-scope-consistency\nstatus: in-progress\ncreated_at: 2026-11-10\ncompleted_at:\nsupersedes:\ndepends_on:\nremediates:\npriority: 1\ncomponents: pose-mcp\ntask_type: bugfix\ndelivers: governance:index-recovery-and-close-scope-consistency\n---\n\n# Spec: O motor aponta o remédio que funciona: índice indisponível, escopo do `close` e limite do módulo\n\n## 1. Intent\n\n### Goal\n\nTrês respostas do motor mandavam o operador para um comando que não resolvia o que ele acabara de tentar. Cada uma vira uma resposta consistente: o que os leitores recomendam (`pose index`) repara; o `pose close` de um escopo que não é spec diz como ele fecha; e o índice de módulos não conta contra o seu limite o registro de governança que cresce a cada fechamento.\n\n### Business value\n\nAchado ao investigar o aviso persistente do harne8 (113 avisos do gate, todos \"review input closure is unknown: index generation does not bind input …\"), 2026-11-10:\n\n1. O harne8 tinha um commit (`e1593bde`) com `spec-graph.json` e `roadmaps.json` regenerados e o `generation.json` não; os leitores respondiam \"does not match generation …; run `pose index`\" e o `pose index` recusava (`index input closure could not be proved … index generation could not be pinned; nothing was written`): a operação que constrói o índice a partir das fontes tratava como desconhecida a geração que ela mesma vai substituir.\n2. Mesmo corrigido isso, o `pose index` do harne8 falhava com \"module discovery walk: governance read view budget exceeded\": o limite de 10 000 entradas da varredura de módulos foi cruzado por 7 319 arquivos rastreados do submódulo `pose-dist` (1 652 bundles e 1 597 atestados de review, 525 specs, …) mais os 2 833 do próprio harne8; o `.pose` de cada projeto não contém módulo e cresce a cada fechamento, então uma instância grande nunca mais atualizaria seus índices. A mensagem era só o nome do orçamento.\n3. `pose close milestone:… --apply` respondia \"the closeout plan covers spec scopes; use `pose close milestone:…` for milestone scopes\": a invocação sugerida era a mesma que o operador digitou, e a diferença (o modo de plano é de spec; os outros escopos fecham sem `--apply`) não estava dita.\n\n### Constraints\n\n- Os leitores continuam recusando um conjunto de índices indisponível (`pose index --verify` e `ReadIndex`): só a operação que reconstrói o conjunto a partir das fontes deixa de tratar a geração indisponível como desconhecimento.\n- A mesma entrada dá a mesma geração: o reparo devolve o conjunto ao que ele seria.\n- Nada de `.pose` entra no inventário de módulos; o resto da varredura, os limites e as exclusões não mudam.\n\n### Non-goals\n\n- Impedir um commit que deixa o conjunto de índices inconsistente (o gate já o aponta como erro no modo estrito; o reparo agora existe).\n- Mudar quem fecha um milestone ou o que `pose close milestone:` faz.\n\n## 2. Requirements\n\n- R1: `pose close <escopo> --plan|--apply|--resume` num escopo que não é spec sai com 2 e diz que o modo é o plano de fechamento de uma spec, que um escopo desse tipo fecha com `pose close <escopo>` sem nenhum modo e que se lê com `pose closeout-check <escopo>`; a mensagem do plano (também pelo MCP) não sugere a invocação recusada.\n- R2: Com a geração publicada indisponível (a cópia legada de um índice não bate com o descritor, ou o diretório da geração não existe), `pose index` publica uma geração nova a partir das fontes, `pose index --verify` passa depois, e a mesma entrada dá a mesma geração; os leitores e o `--verify` antes do reparo continuam reportando indisponível.\n- R3: A varredura de módulos não percorre diretórios `.pose` (do projeto, de um submódulo ou de um projeto aninhado): um registro de governança de qualquer tamanho não conta contra o limite nem entra no inventário (nem os README de `.pose`).\n- R4: Quando o limite da varredura de módulos é excedido por outra coisa, o erro diz o que foi contado (mais de N entradas ou M bytes de caminhos) e que se ignora o que é gerado ou vendorizado no `.gitignore`, e continua sendo o erro de orçamento da visão de leitura.\n\n## 3. Technical Plan\n\n### Affected areas\n\n`pose-mcp/internal/cli` (o `close` e os testes) e `pose-mcp/internal/pose` (a visão de leitura do índice, a varredura de módulos e a mensagem do plano), mais os manuais.\n\n### Artifacts\n\n- created: .pose/specs/2026-11-10-pose-index-recovery-and-close-scope-consistency.md\n- created: .pose/starts/pose-index-recovery-and-close-scope-consistency.json\n- created: .pose/changelogs/unreleased/pose-index-recovery-and-close-scope-consistency.md\n- modified: pose-mcp/internal/cli/closeout_plan.go\n- modified: pose-mcp/internal/pose/closeout_plan.go\n- modified: pose-mcp/internal/pose/governance_read_view.go\n- modified: pose-mcp/internal/pose/module_discovery.go\n- created: pose-mcp/internal/cli/close_plan_nonspec_test.go\n- modified: pose-mcp/internal/cli/index_generation_test.go\n- created: pose-mcp/internal/pose/module_discovery_governance_record_test.go\n- modified: .pose/indexes/validation-matrix.json\n- modified: POSE.md\n- modified: locales/pt-BR/POSE.md\n- modified: pose-mcp/internal/scaffold/dist/POSE.md\n- modified: pose-mcp/internal/scaffold/dist/locales/pt-BR/POSE.md\n\n### Delivery targets\n\n- governance:index-recovery-and-close-scope-consistency module:pose-mcp profile:backend-go entrypoint:pose-mcp/cmd/pose/main.go\n\n### API/contract changes\n\n`pose close` de um escopo que não é spec com um modo de plano passa a sair com 2 (antes saía com 1 por um erro do plano); o repo-map deixa de listar os README de `.pose`; `pose index` repara uma geração indisponível.\n\n## 4. Tasks\n\n- [x] Reproduzir no harne8: o `pose index` recusando a geração indisponível, depois o limite de módulos\n- [x] A operação de reconstrução não trata a geração indisponível como desconhecimento\n- [x] A varredura de módulos não percorre `.pose` e o erro do limite diz o que foi contado\n- [x] A recusa dos modos de plano num escopo que não é spec diz como o escopo fecha\n- [x] Testes que falham sem cada correção (verificado) e manuais\n- [ ] Regenerar os índices do harne8 com o motor corrigido\n- [ ] Revisão independente e fechamento\n\n## 5. Decisions\n\n### Decision D1\n- Basis: R2\n- Minimal option: documentar a restauração manual do arquivo do índice a partir do commit anterior\n- Selected option: a operação que reconstrói o conjunto a partir das fontes não lê a geração publicada, então uma geração indisponível não torna o seu fechamento desconhecido\n- Rationale: os índices são derivados; o comando que as mensagens dos leitores recomendam tem de poder rodar; o publicador já substitui um descritor ilegível, só a visão de captura o impedia\n- Consequences: um conjunto adulterado é substituído por um reconstruído das fontes (o `--verify` o aponta antes); a geração antiga não é preservada se o diretório dela não existe\n- Falsifier: um `pose index` que recusa por causa da geração indisponível, ou uma geração diferente da que as mesmas entradas dão\n\n### Decision D2\n- Basis: R3\n- Minimal option: subir o limite de 10 000 entradas\n- Selected option: não percorrer `.pose`\n- Rationale: o limite alcança de novo qualquer valor fixo, porque o registro cresce a cada fechamento; um diretório que nunca contém módulo não precisa ser contado\n- Consequences: o repo-map perde os README de `.pose`, que são documentos da própria governança\n- Falsifier: um módulo ou arquivo de implantação dentro de `.pose` que o inventário deixa de ver\n\n## 6. Validation\n\n### Strategy\n\nCada teste reproduz um defeito visto no harne8 e falha sem a correção (verificado); o reparo foi também rodado no harne8 real.\n\n### Deterministic checks\n\n`go test` dos testes do rastreio; a matriz estrita em clone limpo.\n\n### Requirement trace\n- R1 [satisfied] test:TestClosePlanModesOnANonSpecScopeNameTheRightInvocation check:index-recovery-integration\n- R2 [satisfied] test:TestIndexRepairsAnUnavailableGenerationThatItsReadersTellYouToRun\n- R3 [satisfied] test:TestModuleDiscoveryDoesNotWalkTheGovernanceRecord\n- R4 [satisfied] test:TestModuleDiscoveryNamesItsBoundWhenItIsExceeded\n\n### Known gaps\n- O motor não impede o commit que deixa o conjunto de índices inconsistente; o gate estrito o aponta e o reparo agora funciona.\n- O erro do plano pelo MCP foi reescrito (a mesma função), sem teste próprio.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Final Report\n\n### Delivered scope\n`pose index` repara uma geração indisponível (R2); a varredura de módulos não percorre `.pose` e diz o seu limite (R3, R4); o `close` de um escopo que não é spec diz como ele fecha (R1).\n\n### Files and modules changed\n- pose-mcp/internal/cli e pose-mcp/internal/pose, os testes e os quatro POSE.md; a matriz ganha `index-recovery-integration`.\n\n### Validation executed\n- Command: `pose validate --strict --jobs 4` em clone limpo do head revisado\n- Result: registrado pela evidência de validação selada; revisão delegada independente concluída com `pose review conclude`.\n\n### Residual risks\n- O repo-map de uma instância muda (sem os README de `.pose`) na primeira regeneração.\n- Uma instância com mais de 10 000 entradas fora de `.pose` continua parando, agora com a mensagem dizendo como ignorar.\n\n### Follow-ups\n\n- [open] Fazer o gate de commit recusar, no modo estrito, um commit que muda um índice sem o descritor da geração. (owner:@pose-maintainers crit:low review:2027-02-08)\ndiff --git a/.pose/changelogs/unreleased/pose-index-recovery-and-close-scope-consistency.md b/.pose/changelogs/unreleased/pose-index-recovery-and-close-scope-consistency.md\nnew file mode 100644\nindex 00000000..84f1e58e\n--- /dev/null\n+++ b/.pose/changelogs/unreleased/pose-index-recovery-and-close-scope-consistency.md\n@@ -0,0 +1,6 @@\n+---\n+spec: pose-index-recovery-and-close-scope-consistency\n+category: fixed\n+breaking: false\n+---\n+`pose index` repara um conjunto de índices cuja geração publicada está indisponível (o comando que os leitores recomendam passa a funcionar); a varredura de módulos não percorre `.pose` (o registro de governança que cresce a cada fechamento já podia estourar o limite de 10 000 entradas) e o erro do limite diz o que foi contado; e `pose close` de um milestone ou roadmap com `--plan`, `--apply` ou `--resume` sai com 2 dizendo que o escopo fecha com `pose close <escopo>` sem modo, em vez de sugerir a invocação recusada.\ndiff --git a/.pose/indexes/repo-map.json b/.pose/indexes/repo-map.json\nindex fef9ad14..0e8cb524 100644\n--- a/.pose/indexes/repo-map.json\n+++ b/.pose/indexes/repo-map.json\n@@ -90,9 +90,6 @@\n   ],\n   \"readmes\": [\n     \".agents/skills/README.md\",\n-    \".pose/assessments/README.md\",\n-    \".pose/knowledge/README.md\",\n-    \".pose/reports/README.md\",\n     \"README.md\",\n     \"README.pt-BR.md\",\n     \"examples/brownfield-kits/README.md\",\ndiff --git a/.pose/indexes/validation-matrix.json b/.pose/indexes/validation-matrix.json\nindex e776a81a..9f51ade3 100644\n--- a/.pose/indexes/validation-matrix.json\n+++ b/.pose/indexes/validation-matrix.json\n@@ -2596,6 +2596,20 @@\n           \"severity\": \"required\",\n           \"evidenceClass\": \"integration\"\n         },\n+        {\n+          \"name\": \"index-recovery-integration\",\n+          \"program\": \"go\",\n+          \"args\": [\n+            \"test\",\n+            \"./internal/cli\",\n+            \"./internal/pose\",\n+            \"-run\",\n+            \"TestClosePlanModesOnANonSpecScope|TestIndexRepairsAnUnavailable|TestModuleDiscoveryDoesNotWalk|TestModuleDiscoveryNamesItsBound\",\n+            \"-count=1\"\n+          ],\n+          \"severity\": \"required\",\n+          \"evidenceClass\": \"integration\"\n+        },\n         {\n           \"name\": \"runtime-qos-integration\",\n           \"program\": \"go\",\ndiff --git a/POSE.md b/POSE.md\nindex 74e61a35..94027ce4 100644\n--- a/POSE.md\n+++ b/POSE.md\n@@ -395,7 +395,7 @@ pose release-package-manifests --version X.Y.Z --checksums <checksums.txt> --out\n - Governed capabilities (spec `pose-governed-capabilities-default-on-new-instances`) — agency readiness, contract nodes, atomic start and causality closeout with the `structural-materiality@1` overlay. A new instance adopts all four: when `pose install` creates `.pose/policy/review.json` it sets their keys, dates atomic start, causality closeout and the overlay with the install day, and logs each adoption. An instance that already has a review policy adopts nothing at install, and `pose update` never adopts, even when it seeds a missing policy. `pose adopt <capability>` previews the keys one capability needs and `--apply` writes them, dated today or `--date YYYY-MM-DD`, so work created before the date is not re-judged; adopting an adopted capability keeps its date, `--off` removes the same keys, and a result the review-policy reader would refuse is never written. `state --governance` names the command for each capability not adopted. Every adoptable capability is in one catalog (spec `pose-capability-catalog`): the four above, criterion reuse, the Definition of Ready, the review overlays (`overlay:<profile>`), qualified references and spec authority transfer (which raise `schema_version` as their contract requires), signed attestations and verified identity. `pose adopt --list` shows each with its state — `on`, `off`, `declined`, `deferred` or `needs-setup` —, what it changes day to day, since which version and whether a new instance gets it (`--json` for the same data); a new instance adopts every capability the catalog marks as a default, the Definition of Ready and criterion reuse included. A capability whose requirement is off, or whose prerequisite (a trusted issuer, an authority audience) is missing, is refused with what is missing; `--off` is refused while another adopted capability depends on it. `pose adopt <capability> --decline --reason <text>` or `--defer --reason <text>` records in `.pose/policy/adoption-decisions.json` that the project looked and chose not to adopt it now, so it is not asked again; adopting later clears it, and the file is never shipped to another instance. A new instance's role map in `.pose/policy/actions.json` names nobody, and `pose doctor` lists naming them as a next step. A fresh install reports no warning in `pose doctor`: milestone and roadmap profiles count only once the project has a roadmap, and evidence-class coverage reads only the required checks of the shipped stacks some module uses (every shipped test and build check declares its class).\n - Governed effects (spec `pose-governed-effect-enforcement`) — opt-in with `agency_readiness_version: 1` in `.pose/policy/review.json`. Once adopted, an unsatisfied action request refuses the transition it restricts at its write point: `pose close` refuses with `close.refused.action-request-pending` and the request's message, `closeout-check` / `pose_closeout_state` report the typed diagnostic and `next_step: answer-action-request`, continuous closeout cannot complete, `pose start --apply` refuses a plan restricted at start (a preview taken before a request was opened is stale at apply), and `pose release prepare` refuses — dry-run included — while a request restricts release of one of the release's specs. Each gate recomputes the restriction when it runs; a declined approval keeps refusing. Only action requests are enforced here: dependencies, review and evidence keep their own gates. Without adoption, requests are shown in Attention and restrict nothing, and `state --governance` reports the capability as supported and not adopted.\n - Spec transfer and action requests (spec `pose-transfer-preserves-obligations`) — `spec-transfer preview` lists the source spec's action requests in `action_requests` with a disposition: `invalidate-in-source` for an open or answered request, `history-only` for one already closed. When the source is retired, each open or answered request receives an invalidation event keyed to the operation, so a resumed transfer records it once; a request opened after the preview makes the plan stale. Requests and answers are bound to their project and grant nothing in the destination, which re-opens a request if it still applies; a same slug and requirement id in two projects never share a request.\n-- `close spec:<slug> --plan|--apply|--resume` (spec `pose-recoverable-closeout-plan`) — the closeout as an ordered, recoverable plan computed from the repository: context (an in-progress spec, no uncommitted change outside the paths the plan writes), evidence and index (validate into `results_path`, then `pose index`, as a pair), seal, mechanical attestation (`--reviewer` names the reviewing execution; never invented), judgment, verify and the guarded transition. `--plan` previews (`--json` too); `--apply [--digest <plan digest>]` refuses a plan that no longer matches the repository, runs the mechanical steps and stops with exit 3 at a pending judgment, listing it with the sealed evidence refs (`closeout_plan.evidence`) and an attest command filled with them, one `--tool` per required tool and one `--criterion` per pending judgment (`closeout_plan.attest`, spec `pose-quickstart-real-lifecycle`); a requirement trace that is incomplete — a requirement with no entry, an orphan or malformed entry, or requirements with no `### Requirement trace` — blocks the plan at its `trace` step, before anything is sealed, and `pose close` refuses the transition for the same reason, so a closed spec never fails the lint that follows; `--resume` re-plans after an interruption, so done steps are not repeated and no bundle or attestation is duplicated. A checkpoint under `.pose/closeout-plans/` records progress and is removed on completion; a removal that fails is reported. It is progress, never a receipt (spec `pose-journal-checkpoint-and-lock-recovery`): `--plan` shows it as `valid`, `corrupt`, `unsupported` or `unavailable`; an apply over one that does not read stops before any step, for a person to inspect and remove it; and one that records an evidence, index, seal, attestation or transition step the repository does not reflect is a conflict, until `--apply --digest <current plan>` starts a new run. The plan never answers a judgment, publishes or edits policy, and promises a recoverable operation, not a transaction over Git and external processes. Operation results (spec `pose-cli-mcp-operation-results`, ADR 2026-10-16): `--apply|--resume --json` writes exactly one JSON document — `operation`, `outcome` (`completed`, `already-satisfied`, `waiting`, `blocked`, `conflict`, `failed`), the `step` it stopped at, `steps_run`, `pending`, `next_action`, the `effects` the domain confirmed, the steps left `unconfirmed` after a failure, `resumable`, a typed `error.cause` and the plan in `data` — and the human output reads the same result. A call that runs no step reports `already-satisfied`, not `closed`: it is not a fresh verification, and `pose closeout-check` is. Two different modes, or `--digest`, `--reviewer` or `--skip-evidence` with `--plan`, exit 2 before anything runs; `pose start` refuses two of `--apply`, `--status` and `--cancel`, `--digest` outside `--apply` and `--cancel --json`; an apply that fails after recording its baseline reports the recorded phase as a confirmed effect and the same `--apply --digest` as its next action, which finishes from the start record a transition already published; a spec that has not moved is judged again by the current plan and its governed restrictions, and a baseline the repository has moved past is reported as a conflict to cancel and preview again, never as resumable; a failed apply lists every state it confirms as an effect (the baseline and, once published, the spec moved to `in-progress`) and in `steps_run` only the steps that call ran, and a start record left unreadable (a baseline write cut short) is reported with the `baseline` step unconfirmed and a next action to inspect and remove it, never repaired automatically. `pose close <scope>` without a plan mode refuses `--json`. An MCP gate (`pose_check`, `pose_lint_spec`, `pose_skills_check`) stopped by its deadline or a cancellation returns `isError` with `error_code: execution_inconclusive`, its cause and partial output, never `passed=false`. The usage journal records a stop on a reviewer as `waiting`, not `failed`, and never averages an unmeasured interval as 0 ms.\n+- `close spec:<slug> --plan|--apply|--resume` (spec `pose-recoverable-closeout-plan`) — the closeout as an ordered, recoverable plan computed from the repository: context (an in-progress spec, no uncommitted change outside the paths the plan writes), evidence and index (validate into `results_path`, then `pose index`, as a pair), seal, mechanical attestation (`--reviewer` names the reviewing execution; never invented), judgment, verify and the guarded transition. `--plan` previews (`--json` too); `--apply [--digest <plan digest>]` refuses a plan that no longer matches the repository, runs the mechanical steps and stops with exit 3 at a pending judgment, listing it with the sealed evidence refs (`closeout_plan.evidence`) and an attest command filled with them, one `--tool` per required tool and one `--criterion` per pending judgment (`closeout_plan.attest`, spec `pose-quickstart-real-lifecycle`); a requirement trace that is incomplete — a requirement with no entry, an orphan or malformed entry, or requirements with no `### Requirement trace` — blocks the plan at its `trace` step, before anything is sealed, and `pose close` refuses the transition for the same reason, so a closed spec never fails the lint that follows; `--resume` re-plans after an interruption, so done steps are not repeated and no bundle or attestation is duplicated. A checkpoint under `.pose/closeout-plans/` records progress and is removed on completion; a removal that fails is reported. It is progress, never a receipt (spec `pose-journal-checkpoint-and-lock-recovery`): `--plan` shows it as `valid`, `corrupt`, `unsupported` or `unavailable`; an apply over one that does not read stops before any step, for a person to inspect and remove it; and one that records an evidence, index, seal, attestation or transition step the repository does not reflect is a conflict, until `--apply --digest <current plan>` starts a new run. The plan never answers a judgment, publishes or edits policy, and promises a recoverable operation, not a transaction over Git and external processes. Operation results (spec `pose-cli-mcp-operation-results`, ADR 2026-10-16): `--apply|--resume --json` writes exactly one JSON document — `operation`, `outcome` (`completed`, `already-satisfied`, `waiting`, `blocked`, `conflict`, `failed`), the `step` it stopped at, `steps_run`, `pending`, `next_action`, the `effects` the domain confirmed, the steps left `unconfirmed` after a failure, `resumable`, a typed `error.cause` and the plan in `data` — and the human output reads the same result. A call that runs no step reports `already-satisfied`, not `closed`: it is not a fresh verification, and `pose closeout-check` is. Two different modes, or `--digest`, `--reviewer` or `--skip-evidence` with `--plan`, exit 2 before anything runs; `pose start` refuses two of `--apply`, `--status` and `--cancel`, `--digest` outside `--apply` and `--cancel --json`; an apply that fails after recording its baseline reports the recorded phase as a confirmed effect and the same `--apply --digest` as its next action, which finishes from the start record a transition already published; a spec that has not moved is judged again by the current plan and its governed restrictions, and a baseline the repository has moved past is reported as a conflict to cancel and preview again, never as resumable; a failed apply lists every state it confirms as an effect (the baseline and, once published, the spec moved to `in-progress`) and in `steps_run` only the steps that call ran, and a start record left unreadable (a baseline write cut short) is reported with the `baseline` step unconfirmed and a next action to inspect and remove it, never repaired automatically. `pose close <scope>` without a plan mode refuses `--json`. An MCP gate (`pose_check`, `pose_lint_spec`, `pose_skills_check`) stopped by its deadline or a cancellation returns `isError` with `error_code: execution_inconclusive`, its cause and partial output, never `passed=false`. The usage journal records a stop on a reviewer as `waiting`, not `failed`, and never averages an unmeasured interval as 0 ms. The plan modes are a spec's: `pose close <scope> --plan|--apply|--resume` on a milestone or a roadmap exits 2 and says so. Any other scope closes with `pose close <scope>` and no plan flag (the guarded transition: it verifies the gate, which on a large repository takes minutes, and names the next closeout, `pose close roadmap:<slug>` once every milestone of a roadmap is closed); `pose closeout-check <scope>` reads whether it is terminal and is what `terminal=true` means for a milestone or a roadmap.\n - Reuse by material equivalence (spec `pose-material-equivalence-reuse`) — a criterion's answer is reused only when every input it depends on is equal in the new bundle: its definition, the independence, the scope sections, the subject entries (all of them for a subject-sensitive criterion), the evidence of the classes it accepts, the governing inputs and its tools; judgment reuse also needs `allow_criterion_reuse`. When a bundle is superseded, `review verify` (`delta.criterion_reuse` in JSON) shows each criterion as `equivalent`, `changed` with the inputs that changed, or `new`, and each reused answer names the attestation it came from. Editing bookkeeping such as the Validation log changes no criterion; a code change names the changed subject.\n - Assessment freshness (spec `pose-adaptive-assessment-freshness`) — a component assessment records its binding: the committed tree of the component, the engine version and the validation-matrix digest. `pose assess discover --if-stale [--component <dir>]` reuses an assessment whose binding is unchanged and refreshes it otherwise, printing `assess.freshness.<component>` with the reason (content changed, engine changed, matrix changed, uncommitted changes, or no binding on an older record). Age alone never stales an assessment. A missing or stale assessment of a component an in-progress spec declares appears as an advisory `evidence` obligation in `state --attention`; it gates nothing.\n - `followups --candidates [--json]` (spec `pose-followup-reconciliation-candidates`) — lists open follow-ups with a reconciliation reason: `target-terminal` (a spec the follow-up names is done, superseded or abandoned), `evidence-present` (a test it names now exists), `overdue` (its review date passed), plus lexical near-duplicate clusters. Each candidate shows its evidence and its limits; nothing is dispositioned, similarity never becomes `duplicate` without judgment, and an open follow-up never leaves Attention without a disposition written in its spec. Attention shows the same reason on the residual-debt item.\ndiff --git a/locales/pt-BR/POSE.md b/locales/pt-BR/POSE.md\nindex 68f0207e..f6d1a973 100644\n--- a/locales/pt-BR/POSE.md\n+++ b/locales/pt-BR/POSE.md\n@@ -399,7 +399,7 @@ pose release-package-manifests --version X.Y.Z --checksums <checksums.txt> --out\n - Capacidades governadas (spec `pose-governed-capabilities-default-on-new-instances`) — agency readiness, contract nodes, atomic start e causality closeout com o overlay `structural-materiality@1`. Uma instância nova adota as quatro: quando `pose install` cria `.pose/policy/review.json`, grava as chaves, data atomic start, causality closeout e o overlay com o dia da instalação e registra cada adoção no log. Uma instância que já tem policy de review não adota nada na instalação, e `pose update` nunca adota, mesmo quando semeia uma policy ausente. `pose adopt <capability>` mostra as chaves de que uma capacidade precisa e `--apply` as grava, datadas com hoje ou `--date YYYY-MM-DD`, para que trabalho criado antes da data não seja rejulgado; adotar uma capacidade já adotada mantém a data, `--off` remove as mesmas chaves, e um resultado que o leitor da policy recusaria nunca é gravado. `state --governance` aponta o comando para cada capacidade não adotada. Toda capacidade adotável está num catálogo único (spec `pose-capability-catalog`): as quatro acima, reuso de critérios, a Definition of Ready, os overlays de review (`overlay:<profile>`), referências qualificadas e transferência de autoridade de spec (que sobem o `schema_version` como o contrato delas exige), atestações assinadas e identidade verificada. `pose adopt --list` mostra cada uma com seu estado — `on`, `off`, `declined`, `deferred` ou `needs-setup` —, o que muda no dia a dia, desde qual versão e se uma instância nova a recebe (`--json` para os mesmos dados); uma instância nova adota toda capacidade que o catálogo marca como padrão, inclusive a Definition of Ready e o reuso de critérios. Capacidade com requisito desligado, ou com pré-requisito faltando (um emissor confiável, uma audience de autoridade), é recusada com o que falta; `--off` é recusado enquanto outra capacidade adotada depender dela. `pose adopt <capability> --decline --reason <texto>` ou `--defer --reason <texto>` registra em `.pose/policy/adoption-decisions.json` que o projeto olhou e decidiu não adotar agora, para não ser perguntado de novo; adotar depois limpa o registro, e o arquivo nunca é enviado a outra instância. O mapa de papéis de uma instância nova em `.pose/policy/actions.json` não nomeia ninguém, e `pose doctor` lista nomeá-los como próximo passo. Uma instalação nova não tem aviso em `pose doctor`: profiles de milestone e roadmap só contam quando o projeto tem roadmap, e a cobertura de classes de evidência lê apenas os checks obrigatórios das stacks distribuídas que algum módulo usa (todo","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false,"persistedOutputPath":"/home/go/.claude/projects/-var-tmp-pose-work-go-pose-review-run-2171335808-worktree/[private-session]/tool-results/bl1t9p3xm.txt","persistedOutputSize":90665},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01CsspFN3dmB3k76ZeivMFt6","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"subcommandResults"}}]}
